/* book Worked example 2: Q: The water-gas shift, CO(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + H₂(g), has K = 1.00 at about 1100 K. Find the equilibrium conversion of CO for a feed of (a) 1 mol CO and 1 mol H₂O, (b) 1 mol CO and 2 mol H₂O. Take ideal gases. Does the pressure matter?
   1. Σν = 1 + 1 − 1 − 1 = 0, so K = Ky (P/P°)0 = Ky, and nT cancels: K = nCO₂ nH₂/(nCO nH₂O). The pressure does not enter.
   2. (a) nCO = 1 − ξ, nH₂O = 1 − ξ, nCO₂ = nH₂ = ξ. K = ξ²/(1 − ξ)² = 1.00, so ξ/(1 − ξ) = 1 and ξ = 0.500 mol.
   3. (b) nH₂O = 2 − ξ. K = ξ²/[(1 − ξ)(2 − ξ)] = 1.00, so ξ² = 2 − 3ξ + ξ², giving 3ξ = 2 and ξ = 0.667 mol.
   4. Composition in (b), nT = 3 mol: yCO = 0.333/3 = 0.111, yH₂O = 1.333/3 = 0.444, yCO₂ = yH₂ = 0.667/3 = 0.222. Check: (0.222)²/(0.111 × 0.444) = 1.00.
   Answer: CO conversion is 50.0 % with equimolar steam and 66.7 % with twice as much steam; pressure has no effect because Σν = 0. Excess of a cheap reactant (steam) pushes the conversion of the valuable one (CO) up. */
