/* book Worked example: Q: For A ⇌ 2B, K = 0.222 at 400 K. A feed of 1 mol A at 1 bar contains no B. Take ideal gas behaviour and standard pressure 1 bar. (a) Derive Q as a function of extent ξ. (b) Compute Q at ξ = 0.10 mol and ξ = 0.30 mol; state the spontaneous direction. (c) Find equilibrium extent, conversion of A and yield of B.
   1. Stoichiometric table: nA = 1 − ξ mol, nB = 2ξ mol, total nT = 1 + ξ mol.
   2. Mole fractions: yA = (1 − ξ)/(1 + ξ), yB = 2ξ/(1 + ξ).
   3. At P = 1 bar and P° = 1 bar, ideal-gas activities are aA = yA and aB = yB.
   4. Q = aB²/aA = [(2ξ/(1 + ξ))²]/[(1 − ξ)/(1 + ξ)] = 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²).
   5. At ξ = 0.10 mol: Q = 4(0.10)²/(1 − 0.10²) = 0.0404. This is below K = 0.222, so ΔG < 0 and the reaction goes forward.
   6. At ξ = 0.30 mol: Q = 4(0.30)²/(1 − 0.30²) = 0.3956 ≈ 0.396. This is above K, so ΔG > 0 and the reaction goes backward.
   7. At equilibrium: 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²) = 0.222, so 4ξ² = 0.222 − 0.222ξ², giving 4.222ξ² = 0.222 and ξ² = 0.05258.
   8. ξeq = 0.2293 mol. Conversion of A: X = ξ/1 = 0.2293, or 22.9%.
   9. Yield of B: nB,formed/nA0 = 2ξ/1 = 0.4586 ≈ 0.459 mol B per mol A fed.
   Answer: Q(0.10) = 0.0404 < K, so ΔG < 0 and the reaction goes forward; Q(0.30) = 0.396 > K, so ΔG > 0 and it goes backward. At equilibrium ξ = 0.2293 mol, the conversion of A is 0.229 (22.9 %) and the yield of B is 0.459 mol B per mol A fed. */
